Without taxing residents, shall the measure be adopted, updating Costa Mesa’s 41-year-old business license tax, ensuring large businesses pay their proportionate share and helping fund general city services including keeping public areas safe/clean; preventing crime/burglaries; addressing homelessness; maintaining Costa Mesa’s financial stability; revising the tax rate to 50¢ per $1,000 gross receipts, with no increase for small businesses; generating approximately $5,600,000 annually until ended by voters, requiring audits, spending disclosure, funds locally controlled? 

What your vote means 

YES NO 

A “yes” vote would approve reforming the business license tax, leaving the tax unchanged for small businesses and establishing an updated rate for businesses earning over $500,000 annually, raising $4.6 million annually to support crime prevention, police and fire services, homelessness prevention, roads, sidewalks and parks and other general city services, and to help the City maintain fiscal stability. A ”no” vote would leave the existing business license tax in place and maintain the existing $200 cap adopted in 1985. 

For and against 

FOR AGAINST 

